WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.819 --> 00:00:06.390
The SQL keywords you learned for querying
are harmless as they make no changes to your

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.700
existing data.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.710
Now, we will cover some keywords that do change
data.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.169
Changing data can be both good and bad.

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:18.590
It’s good in the sense of using keywords
to add, change, and remove data, but we want

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:23.330
to make sure only those who know how to use
these keywords can.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:27.260
The main reason: SQL doesn’t feature an
undo button.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:32.169
Once you change data, it’s changed.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:36.350
Our next goal in working with queries is to
insert data into an entity.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.120
There are two basic ways to do this: We can
either copy records from one table to another

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.960
or insert records manually or from a data
source such as an app.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.609
In the first example, we see a query that
inserts all the records from the BikeRepairs

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:56.200
table into the BikeRepairsTest table.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:02.370
I will execute this query, and the records
from the BikeRepairs table have been copied

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.290
into the BikeRepairsTest table.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:10.860
INSERT INTO with a SELECT is used to copy
records from one table to an existing table.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:16.549
But, what if you want to copy records from
an existing table into a new table.

00:01:16.549 --> 00:01:21.710
A SELECT INTO statement will make this happen.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:27.579
Take a look at this statement that selects
everything into a BikeRepairsArchive table

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:30.350
from the BikeRepairs table.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.380
The BikeRepairsArchive table does not yet
exist.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:40.700
However, once I select and execute this statement,
the table will exist with the records from

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.509
the BikeRepairs table.

00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:48.219
SELECT INTO is a great option to use when
one wants to make a copy of an entire table,

00:01:48.219 --> 00:01:50.700
including the table's structure.

